  <h2>1. Clean and inspect</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Wash the panel with automotive shampoo and clean water, then dry it with a clean microfiber towel.</li>
    <li>Inspect in bright, indirect light from several angles.</li>
    <li>Determine whether the white mark is transferred material, a clear-coat scratch, exposed primer, or bare metal.</li>
    <li>Do not repeatedly rub a dry or dirty surface; this can add scratches.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>2. Choose the repair approach</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Appearance</th><th>Likely approach</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>White mark sits on top of the paint and transfers to a paint-safe microfiber/cleaner</td><td>Careful removal of the transfer material; re-inspect the underlying finish.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Scratch is visible but does not catch a fingernail</td><td>May be in the clear coat. A paint-safe polish may improve it, but test a small area and avoid removing excessive clear coat.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fingernail catches in the scratch</td><td>May extend through the clear coat. Touch-up or professional spot repair may be more appropriate than polishing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Primer or bare metal is visible</td><td>Protect the exposed area and arrange a compatible primer/basecoat/clearcoat repair promptly to prevent corrosion.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>3. Match the paint</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Find the factory paint code on the vehicle identification label.</li>
    <li>Order by paint code, not just the name Denim Blue.</li>
    <li>Check whether the product is intended for a metallic finish and whether it includes the required basecoat and clearcoat steps.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Avoid making it worse</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Do not use household cleaners, abrasive pads, or aggressive compounds.</li>
    <li>Do not wet-sand unless you understand paint thickness and have the proper materials and experience.</li>
    <li>Do not apply touch-up paint over dirt, wax, or loose corrosion.</li>
    <li>Follow the paint product's temperature, curing, and application instructions. Volvo's 2021 S60 touch-up guidance specifies a clean, dry surface and a surface temperature of at least 15 °C (59 °F).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Job notes</h2>
  <p>Record the panel, scratch length and depth, paint code, products used, preparation, number of coats, and curing time. Photograph the damage before and after in the same lighting.</p>
  <h2>2026 paint repair cost guide (U.S.)</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Repair level</th><th>Estimated cost</th><th>When it fits</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th>DIY cleaner / polish</th><td>$15–$60 in supplies</td><td>Paint transfer or very light clear-coat marks; no exposed primer or metal.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>DIY touch-up kit</th><td>$30–$100</td><td>Small chips or narrow scratches after confirming the factory paint code.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Professional localized repair</th><td>$150–$600</td><td>Small area where blending and paint condition allow a spot repair.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Full panel refinish</th><td>$500–$1,500+</td><td>Deep or widespread damage, exposed metal, or color-match/blending work.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="photo-disclaimer">These are broad 2026 U.S. budgeting allowances, not model-specific estimates; body-shop quotes vary with panel size, metallic paint matching, damage depth and adjacent-panel blending. Ask whether corrosion treatment, primer, basecoat, clearcoat and blending are included.</p>

  <h2>Volvo identification and repair reference</h2>
  <p>See Volvo's <a href="https://www.volvocars.com/en-ca/support/car/s60/21w46/article/1f2a2f75c7fab5e2c0a81f6f0da34d7b/bc2f936bc7faf93ac0a81f6f20241412/96fc5cee8c30fa4cc0a8cc721d5725d5/b57844468e30863ec0a801517ae3bb4e/">2021 S60 minor paint-damage guidance</a> for its touch-up sequence and surface-temperature requirement. Volvo also links an explanatory stone-chip illustration; it is a diagram, not a photograph of damage on an S60.</p>
  <p>Use the vehicle's own product/identification label to confirm the paint code and any secondary color code. Volvo's <a href="https://www.volvocars.com/en-ca/support/car/s60/20w17/article/b0804d54c7fc096bc0a81f6f065ad63e_f512e0397febd1b3c0a801514a72cbd0/">2021 S60 type-designations and label guide</a> explains the identification decals and notes that U.S. and Canadian label placement differs. Do not order touch-up paint from the color name alone.</p>

</BaseLayout>
